// Comprehensive demonstration of the `diagram` package — node-and-edge diagrams drawn
// entirely in the document language over the \picture vector layer. Every node shape,
// both placement modes, every edge kind, the arrowhead styles and a couple of complete
// realistic diagrams are exercised below.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/diagram-demo.script

\use{document}
\use{diagram}

\def headline {\italic{The diagram package} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\title{The diagram Package}
\author{A tour of nodes, shapes and routed edges}
\maketitle

A \italic{node} is a named, measured box of some shape; an \italic{edge} is an arrow
between two named nodes. Each node is placed at an absolute coordinate or relative to a
node already drawn, and every edge attaches to the true boundary of each node — so an
arrow, straight or routed, always meets the outline cleanly whatever the shapes. A whole
diagram lives inside one \italic{\\picture} box that flows in the text like any other.

\section{Node shapes}

The package recognises nine shapes. Each one sizes itself to its label and the configured
padding, then clamps to a minimum width and height. The default shape is \italic{box}; the
rest are selected with the optional argument, as in \italic{\\node[diamond]\{name\}\{…\}\{…\}}.

\subsection{The rectangular family}

\italic{box}, \italic{round} (a rounded rectangle), \italic{stadium} (fully rounded ends),
\italic{parallelogram} (sheared by the configured slant) and \italic{hexagon} (pointed
ends) all clip an edge to a rectangle, so arrows between them meet a flat side squarely.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:5.4in height:1.1in {
  \node{box}{at 0.45in 0.55in}{box}
  \node[round]{round}{right box 26}{round}
  \node[stadium]{stad}{right round 26}{stadium}
  \node[parallelogram]{para}{right stad 26}{parallel}
  \node[hexagon]{hex}{right para 26}{hexagon}
}}

\subsection{The rounded family}

\italic{ellipse}, \italic{circle} and \italic{diamond} clip an edge by a smooth or
diamond outline. A circle grows to its label's diagonal; a diamond is inflated so the text
fits inside the rhombus. The ninth shape, \italic{subroutine}, is a box with two inner
side bars (the flowchart symbol for a predefined process).

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:5.4in height:1.3in {
  \node[ellipse]{ell}{at 0.55in 0.65in}{ellipse}
  \node[circle]{cir}{right ell 30}{circle}
  \node[diamond]{dia}{right cir 30}{ok?}
  \node[subroutine]{sub}{right dia 30}{subroutine}
}}

\section{Placement}

A node is positioned either by an absolute coordinate or relative to another node.

\subsection{Absolute coordinates}

\italic{at X Y} takes two scalar expressions (which may carry units, e.g. \italic{at 1in
0.5in}); \italic{at (X,Y)} takes one space-free parenthesised point. Absolute placement
lets nodes form an arbitrary graph rather than a single row.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:3.0in height:1.5in {
  \node{n1}{at 0.4in 1.15in}{A}
  \node{n2}{at (2.1in,1.15in)}{B}
  \node{n3}{at 1.25in 0.35in}{C}
  \edge{n1}{n2}
  \edge{n1}{n3}
  \edge{n2}{n3}
}}

\subsection{Relative placement}

\italic{below}, \italic{above}, \italic{right} and \italic{left} place a node a true gap
away from a reference node's boundary — the gap is measured between the two outlines, so
neighbours sit a clean distance apart whatever their sizes. An optional trailing number
overrides the default gap.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:1.7in {
  \node{c}{at 2.3in 0.85in}{centre}
  \node{up}{above c}{above}
  \node{dn}{below c}{below}
  \node{lf}{left c}{left}
  \node{rt}{right c 50}{right (gap 50)}
  \edge{c}{up}
  \edge{c}{dn}
  \edge{c}{lf}
  \edge{c}{rt}
}}

\section{Edges}

Every kind of edge leaves and enters through a node's boundary. The straight, undirected,
two orthogonal, curved, self-loop and entry-stub variants are shown together below.

\subsection{Straight and undirected}

\italic{\\edge} draws a straight arrow with an optional label; \italic{\\link} draws an
undirected connector with no arrowhead — for relationships that have no direction.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:0.9in {
  \node{a}{at 0.45in 0.45in}{A}
  \node{b}{right a 60}{B}
  \node{d}{right b 70}{C}
  \node{e}{right d 60}{D}
  \edge[edge]{a}{b}
  \link[link]{d}{e}
}}

\subsection{Orthogonal routes}

\italic{\\edgehv} routes horizontally then vertically (TikZ's \italic{-|}), and
\italic{\\edgevh} vertically then horizontally (\italic{|-}) — the natural routes for a
decision's side branch or a feedback line. Each turns at the corner and still enters its
target's boundary cleanly.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:1.7in {
  \node{p}{at 0.6in 1.25in}{P}
  \node{q}{at 2.5in 0.35in}{Q}
  \node{r}{at 0.6in 0.35in}{R}
  \node{s}{at 3.9in 1.25in}{S}
  \edgehv[hv]{p}{q}
  \edgevh[vh]{r}{s}
}}

\subsection{Curved edges and self-loops}

\italic{\\cedge} bows an arrow sideways by a \italic{bend} (positive bows left of the
from→to direction). A pair of \italic{\\cedge}s with the same bend in opposite directions
separate into two arcs — the usual picture for a pair of mutual transitions.
\italic{\\loop} draws a self-loop returning to a node.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.4in height:1.7in {
  \node[circle]{p}{at 1.1in 0.85in}{P}
  \node[circle]{q}{right p 70}{Q}
  \cedge[30]{p}{q}{go}
  \cedge[30]{q}{p}{back}
  \loop{p}{stay}
}}

\subsection{Entry stubs}

\italic{\\dgentry} draws a short stub arrow pointing into a side of a node from outside,
the way a flowchart marks its start or an automaton marks its initial state. The direction
is \italic{north}, \italic{south}, \italic{east} or \italic{west} and the length is given
in points.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.4in height:1.0in {
  \node[circle]{s0}{at 1.0in 0.5in}{q0}
  \node[circle]{s1}{right s0 60}{q1}
  \dgentry{s0}{west}{24}
  \edge[a]{s0}{s1}
}}

\section{Arrowhead styles}

The edge arrowhead is set by \italic{\\dgarrow}; the package ships \italic{stealth} (the
default), \italic{triangle}, \italic{bar} and \italic{dot}. The size is \italic{\\dgarrowsize}.
Each setting below is made inside a group so it reverts afterward.

\vskip 8pt

{\set dgminw {30}
\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:0.8in {
  {\set dgarrow {stealth}
   \node{h1a}{at 0.3in 0.4in}{}\node{h1b}{right h1a 6}{stealth}\edge{h1a}{h1b}}
  {\set dgarrow {triangle}
   \node{h2a}{right h1b 22}{}\node{h2b}{right h2a 6}{triangle}\edge{h2a}{h2b}}
  {\set dgarrow {bar}
   \node{h3a}{right h2b 22}{}\node{h3b}{right h3a 6}{bar}\edge{h3a}{h3b}}
  {\set dgarrow {dot}
   \node{h4a}{right h3b 22}{}\node{h4b}{right h4a 6}{dot}\edge{h4a}{h4b}}
}}}

\section{Styling}

Every visual property is a configuration variable set with \italic{\\set name \{value\}}
after \italic{\\use}: the fill colour (\italic{\\dgfill}), the ink for outlines and edges
(\italic{\\dglinecolor}), line widths, the label typeface and size, padding, and the
default gap. Set inside a group, the change applies only to the diagram in that group.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:5.0in height:1.2in {
  {\set dgfill {#fde8e8}\set dglinecolor {#9b2c2c}\set dgfont {12}
   \node[round]{warn}{at 1.0in 0.6in}{Warning}}
  {\set dgfill {#e6fffa}\set dglinecolor {#234e52}\set dgfont {12}
   \node[round]{ok}{right warn 60}{All clear}}
  {\set dglinecolor {#9b2c2c}\set dgarrow {triangle}
   \edge[escalate]{warn}{ok}}
}}

\eject

\section{A complete diagram: a feedback control loop}

The classic block diagram. A reference $r$ enters a summing junction, the error drives a
controller and plant, and the measured output feeds back with a negative sign. The forward
path is straight edges; the feedback path is two orthogonal edges that route around the
diagram and rejoin the summing junction.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:5.4in height:2.4in {
  \node{ref}{at 0.4in 1.7in}{$r$}
  \node[circle]{sum}{right ref 30}{$\Sigma$}
  \node{ctrl}{right sum 40}{Controller $C$}
  \node{plant}{right ctrl 44}{Plant $P$}
  \node{out}{right plant 44}{$y$}
  \node{sensor}{below plant 56}{Sensor $H$}

  \edge{ref}{sum}
  \edge[$e$]{sum}{ctrl}
  \edge[$u$]{ctrl}{plant}
  \edge{plant}{out}
  \edgevh[measure]{out}{sensor}
  \edgehv{sensor}{sum}
  \at anchor:east {1.16in 1.42in}{$-$}
}}

\section{A complete diagram: a data pipeline}

Shapes carry meaning by convention: a stadium for the source and sink, a parallelogram for
I/O, a diamond for a decision and plain boxes for the transforms between them. The chain is
straight edges with one labelled branch routed down to the discard node.

\vskip 10pt

{\set dgfont {10}\set dggap {18}
\centerline{\picture width:6in height:1.8in {
  \node[stadium]{src}{at 0.25in 1.15in}{Source}
  \node[parallelogram]{read}{right src}{Read}
  \node{parse}{right read}{Parse}
  \node[diamond]{okq}{right parse}{ok?}
  \node{agg}{right okq}{Reduce}
  \node[stadium]{sink}{right agg}{Store}
  \node{drop}{below okq 30}{Discard}

  \edge{src}{read}
  \edge{read}{parse}
  \edge{parse}{okq}
  \edge[yes]{okq}{agg}
  \edge{agg}{sink}
  \edgevh[no]{okq}{drop}
}}}

\section{A complete diagram: a turnstile automaton}

Two states with circular outlines, the initial one marked by an entry stub, mutual
transitions drawn as a separated pair of curved edges, and a self-loop where the machine
stays put. This is the whole vocabulary of a small state machine in one picture.

\vskip 10pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:2.0in {
  \node[circle]{locked}{at 1.2in 1.0in}{Locked}
  \node[circle]{open}{right locked 90}{Open}
  \dgentry{locked}{west}{26}
  \cedge[28]{locked}{open}{coin}
  \cedge[28]{open}{locked}{push}
  \loop{locked}{push}
  \loop{open}{coin}
}}

Every figure in this document is one \italic{\\picture} box flowing in the text, set by the
same engine as the prose around it.
